HTTP client-server architecture is a communication model in which a client sends an HTTP request containing a URI, headers, and optionally a payload to a server. The server processes the request, interacts with the database when required, and returns an HTTP response containing a status code, response headers, and optionally a response payload. HTTP methods such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE define the operation to be performed.

