Different types of variables and scope:
Global Variables: Those variables which can be used all through the workspace, which is in all the collections and folders of the workspace, are global variables.
Collection Variables: Those variables which can be used all through the collection in which they are created, in all the requests and folders are collection variables.
Environment Variables: The variables which can be used all throughout the workspace in a particular environment in which they are created are environment variables.
Local Variables: The variables which can be used in a particular request are called local variables.
Data Variables: When we do data-driven testing, the variables used at that time are called data-driven variables. We will study further.

API chaining :
This diagram shows a chained API test flow for user CRUD operations, where the output of one request feeds the next. Here's how it reads:

1. Create User (POST)

Request body: name, gender, email, status
Response gives back a generated id, which is the key piece that drives everything else.

2. Get User (GET)

Uses the id from Create as the path parameter.
Response returns id, name, email.
Compare: the returned values are validated against the original payload you sent in Create (name and email should match). Note that gender and status aren't in the Get response shown here, so only the fields that come back can be compared.

3. Update User (PUT/PATCH)

Uses the same id.
Request body: name, email, status (the fields being modified).

4. Delete User (DELETE)

Uses the same id to remove the record.

The key idea: the id is captured once from the Create response and reused across Get, Update and Delete, so each test depends on the data created by the previous one. In Postman you'd store it in a variable (pm.environment.set("userId", pm.response.json().id)), and in REST Assured you'd extract it from the response and pass it along.

Good validations to add at each step:

Create: status code 201, id is present and not null
Get: status code 200, response fields match the Create payload
Update: status code 200, updated fields reflect the new values; a follow-up Get confirms persistence
Delete: status code 204 (or 200), and a follow-up Get returns 404

This pattern looks like the GoRest-style user API commonly used in API automation training. If you'd like, I can write this flow out as a Postman collection with scripts, or as REST Assured/Python code.

Data Driven Testing: Data-driven testing in API testing with Postman involves running the same test scenario with multiple sets of input data through an external file. The external file will be csv here as it is the most preferred.

